+# Chaudhury et al. 2018 - heavy metals in Dioscorea alata tubers
+
+Chaudhury and coauthors analyzed the tuber of Dioscorea alata, a wild edible yam consumed by the Lodha Tribal community in West Bengal, India. The source reports proximate composition, minerals, vitamins, and toxic heavy metals. It is routeable to yam/root-tuber vegetable evidence, not mineral water.
+
+## Key numbers
+
+Table 1 reports toxic heavy metals in the tuber as ug/g, with each value averaged from three experiments:
+
+| Metal | Reported value |
+|---|---:|
+| Pb | 0.261 +/- 0.0008 ug/g |
+| tAs | 1.221 +/- 0.0008 ug/g |
+| Cd | BDL |
+| tHg | 0.493 +/- 0.004 ug/g |
+
+The same table reports high macromineral values for the tuber, including potassium 39.329 +/- 0.155 mg/g and calcium 30.321 +/- 0.250 mg/g, but those are nutritional minerals rather than HMTc analytes.
+
+## Methods (brief)
+
+The authors combined proximate analysis, mineral analysis, HPLC quantification of phenolic acids and vitamins, and toxic-heavy-metal estimation. The PDF does not state speciation for arsenic or mercury; these are treated as total arsenic and total mercury.
+
+## Implications
+
+Certification: Provides India-specific occurrence context for yam/root-tuber vegetables, but the n is analytical repetitions rather than a market sample survey.
+
+Courses: Useful as an example of a nutritionally framed edible-plant paper that still contains routeable heavy-metal values.
+
+App: Can support a low-confidence yam context value after synthesis.
